# /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/dendrite.conf
#
# Provisions /run/dendrite independently of any single systemd unit's
# lifecycle. dendrited.service and dendrite-magi.service both put their Unix
# sockets here and used to each declare their own `RuntimeDirectory=dendrite`
# pointing at the same shared name — but systemd removes a
# RuntimeDirectory= when the *first* unit referencing it stops, even while
# sibling units using the same directory are still running (see
# https://github.com/systemd/systemd/issues/5394, still open). In practice
# that meant `systemctl restart dendrite-magi` alone would delete
# `/run/dendrite` out from under `dendrited`, unlinking its still-listening
# socket and leaving `dendrite-cli` unable to reconnect until every unit
# sharing the directory happened to restart too.
#
# A tmpfiles.d-provisioned directory has no such lifecycle tie to any one
# unit: it's created once at boot (and re-created if missing whenever
# `systemd-tmpfiles --create` runs, e.g. via postinst on install/upgrade),
# and neither service's own start/stop cycle touches it. See
# packaging/postinst for the `systemd-tmpfiles --create` call that applies
# this on install, and dendrited.service/dendrite-magi.service's own
# comments for why they no longer declare `RuntimeDirectory=`.
#
# dendrite-guard.service is NOT one of these any more: it now runs as its
# own dedicated user (see crates/dendrite-guard/README.md's "Privilege
# separation" section) with its own uniquely-named
# `RuntimeDirectory=dendrite-guard` (/run/dendrite-guard) declared directly
# on that unit — safe there specifically because nothing else references
# that name, so the shared-directory teardown bug above doesn't apply.
d /run/dendrite 0750 dendrite dendrite -
